---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Mockups — Varve Docs"
  description="Apply editable mockup templates, replace linked artwork, adjust surfaces, and choose an export format that preserves the composition."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Mockups
      </nav>
      <h1 class="site-page-title">Mockups</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        A mockup is an editable presentation object with one or more replaceable surfaces. Live
        surfaces follow their source artwork; a snapshot freezes the current appearance into an
        embedded image.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Apply and update a mockup</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Select the artwork to present, then choose <strong>Apply Mockup…</strong> from the Object menu, canvas context menu, command palette, or <strong>Resources &gt; Mockups</strong>.</li>
          <li>Select the mockup object and use its Inspector section to replace a surface, edit its source, snapshot it, reconnect it, or clear it.</li>
          <li>Adjust fit, alignment, rotation, flips, shadow, or numeric geometry. Select a surface chip to edit its corners or edges on the canvas; mesh surfaces expose grid vertices. Press Escape to abort a geometry edit.</li>
          <li>Use <strong>Clip from selection</strong> or <strong>Occluder from selection</strong> when a bezel, hand, or fold should cover part of the placed artwork.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          To author a reusable photo template, select the base photo and choose{' '}
          <strong>Create Mockup Template from Selection…</strong>. The photo stays as the untouched
          base plate while you define surfaces, then save and reuse the template.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export the composed result</h2>
        <p>
          PNG, JPEG, WebP, SVG, and screen-PDF export compose the mockup at the requested output
          scale. SVG includes a rasterized composition for the mockup surface, so that part is not
          editable vector artwork. PDF/X stops with a clear message; flatten the mockup before using
          that print route. React, Flutter, and SwiftUI code exports warn that mockups are outside
          their vector-structure output.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>What the surface model does not do</h2>
        <p>
          Surfaces support flat placement, four-corner perspective, bounded front-facing cylindrical
          arcs, and bounded folded-mesh envelopes. These are 2D mappings, not a full 3D renderer:
          there are no cameras, hidden backsides, lighting solve, calibrated displacement maps, or
          Photoshop smart-object re-rendering. Unsupported cases are reported rather than silently
          approximated. PSD smart objects are detected and reported; they are not re-rendered as
          linked mockups.
        </p>
        <p>
          See the <a href={sitePath('/features/mockups')}>Mockups feature page</a> for template and
          surface details, and <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/export')}>Export</a> for other format
          boundaries.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
